# 6. P1 — Enforce immutable monotonic playlist revisions in Firestore rules

File:

`firestore.rules`

Related:

`firestore.test.js`

The application repository performs revision checks, but Firestore rules currently allow an established revision to be omitted from an incoming update.

The existing `isMonotonicPlaylistUpdate()` logic includes optional checks that can bypass comparison when `syncRevision` is missing.

That weakens the server-side invariant.

## Requirements

Once a playlist document has a `syncRevision`:

- Every subsequent update must include it.
- It must be an integer.
- It must not decrease.
- It must not be removed.
- A deleted playlist can only be recreated with a strictly higher revision.
- Same-revision conflicts must follow the documented policy.
- Deletion updates must preserve monotonic version semantics.
- Snapshot publication must reference a valid committed snapshot.
- Snapshot state transitions must not permit downgrading or publication after deletion has been claimed.

Ensure the Firestore rules and application repository enforce the same state machine.

Do not weaken owner-only authorization or schema validation.

## Required emulator tests

- Missing revision on existing playlist update.
- Revision removed from an existing document.
- Lower revision update.
- Same revision update.
- Higher revision update.
- Stale recreation after deletion.
- Valid higher-revision recreation.
- Invalid tombstone transition.
- Snapshot downgrade from `COMMITTED`.
- Attempted publication of a `DELETING` snapshot.
- Unauthorized cross-user access.
- Unauthenticated access.

Run all tests against the Firestore emulator.

